A library's returned books service publishes return events to many independent components (e.g., inventory updater, patron notifier, analytics). The service keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(ReturnEvent event) {
    for (ReturnListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onReturn(event);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in a library management system is written like this and is read from every request handler:

```java
public class AppConfig {
    private static AppConfig instance;
    private AppConfig() { }
    public static AppConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new AppConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A return processing engine fixes the order of steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class ReturnProcessor {
    public final Receipt process() {
        ReturnInfo info = load();
        ReturnInfo validated = validateReturn(info);
        return write(applyFine(validated));
    }
    protected ReturnInfo load() { return ReturnInfo.fromSystem(); }
    protected abstract ReturnInfo validateReturn(ReturnInfo info);
    protected abstract Receipt applyFine(ReturnInfo info);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A library's returned books processing tool sorts returned items with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<ReturnItem> input); }
public class BubbleSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class MergeSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<ReturnItem> items; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the items and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `BubbleSort` and `MergeSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer rejects the following class:

```java
public class ReturnService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void processReturn(Return ret) { /* ... */ mailer.send(ret.patronEmail(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `ReturnService` names a concrete low-level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two-word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
